<Docs
	page="recipes/ruby"
	title="Ruby"
	lede="Heredocs passed to ActiveRecord, the pg gem, or sqlite3, via tree-sitter-ruby loaded from its release."
>
	<section>
		<h2>Loading the grammar</h2>
		<p>
			squill doesn&rsquo;t compile Ruby in, so the rule names the grammar by its
			release URL. The first run downloads it and records its SHA-256 in a{" "}
			<code>squill.lock</code> beside the config; commit that, and run CI with{" "}
			<code>squill fmt --locked</code>. A loaded grammar has no default query,
			so every rule brings one. See{" "}
			<a href="docs/embedded/#grammars">bringing your own grammar</a> for how
			both work.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>Which strings squill rewrites</h2>
		<p>
			Squiggly heredocs (<code>&lt;&lt;~SQL</code>) are the form to use. Ruby
			strips their common indentation, so squill can lay the query out at the
			statement&rsquo;s indent and the string still means the same SQL.{" "}
			<code>&lt;&lt;-SQL</code> and a bare <code>&lt;&lt;SQL</code> work too; a
			bare heredoc&rsquo;s terminator has to stay in the first column, and
			squill leaves it there.
		</p>
		<p>
			A heredoc&rsquo;s body isn&rsquo;t inside the call in Ruby&rsquo;s tree;
			it follows the whole statement. So the queries below match the statement,
			then the body that comes right after it, and they only cover a heredoc
			passed first (or first in an array). A body with{" "}
			<code>#&lbrace;&rbrace;</code> interpolation or an escape is more than one
			node, and never matches. Pass values as binds instead. A single-quoted
			heredoc (<code>&lt;&lt;~'SQL'</code>) takes no escapes, and the queries
			have a pattern of their own for it that{" "}
			<a href="docs/embedded/#properties">tells squill so</a>, so a backslash in
			one is just a backslash.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>ActiveRecord and pg</h2>
		<p>
			One query covers ActiveRecord&rsquo;s raw-SQL methods (migrations&rsquo;{" "}
			<code>execute</code>, <code>find_by_sql</code>, the connection&rsquo;s{" "}
			<code>select_*</code> and <code>exec_*</code>) and the pg gem&rsquo;s{" "}
			<code>exec</code> family. ActiveRecord binds <code>?</code> placeholders,
			so the rule turns on <code>question-params</code>. Leave it off if you
			only use <code>$1</code>, since in Postgres <code>?</code> is also a jsonb
			operator.
		</p>
		<Recipe name="ruby-activerecord" lang="ruby" />
		<p>
			Rails records which migrations have run by version, not by content, so
			reformatting an old one is safe.
		</p>
	</section>

